-We propose NeST-Com, a digital communication framework that connects next-scale source descriptions, channel protection and prefix-constrained reconstruction under a fixed per-image symbol budget. Its central design problem is matching image-dependent source lengths to protected-frame capacity: a whole residual map may not fit even when many of its tokens would. This motivates finer transmission endpoints within the progressive description~\cite{chande}.
+A fixed per-image digital frame couples the termination point to the actual entropy-code length, signaling and channel-protection overhead. Whole-scale increments can leave capacity unused when the next residual map does not fit, motivating finer endpoints~\cite{chande}. Transmitting part of that map also requires the receiver to incorporate the recovered information into both the residual reconstruction and the context for subsequent scales. The design must therefore connect source termination, protected-frame fitting and progressive reconstruction.
